input  01 02 03 04   32 bits, and 32 is not a multiple of 5

   RFC 4648 standard   AEBAGBA=
   RFC 4648 base32hex  0410610=
   z-base-32 symbols   yrbygby

The first three are ONE encoding with three symbol tables. Same bit
cutting, same padding, same length -- you can convert between them with
a 32-entry lookup and nothing else. The alphabet is a presentation
choice: base32hex sorts in the same order as the data, z-base-32 drops
the characters people mistype.

CROCKFORD IS THE ODD ONE, AND HERE IS THE PROOF
   as a NUMBER            16909060  = 0x1020304
   Crockford (base 32 of that number)   G40R4
   RFC 4648 of the same bytes           AEBAGBA=

They are not re-tablings of each other, and the reason is which end gets
padded. RFC 4648 cuts the BIT STREAM from the left and zero-fills the
last group on the RIGHT, then marks it with '='. Crockford is a notation
for an INTEGER, so leading zeros are meaningless and any padding is on
the LEFT -- there is no '=' because there is no partial group, only a
smaller number.

So the two agree exactly when the input is a whole number of five-bit
pieces and disagree otherwise:
   0000000000   40 bits, 0 left over -> same shape
   01020304     32 bits, 2 left over -> DIFFERENT ANSWERS

Which is why 'Base32' on a converter menu is not a specification. Ask
which alphabet, and ask whether the thing being encoded is a byte string
or a number -- those are two different questions and only one of them is
about symbols.
